course: geography
lesson: 15
title: The Arctic and Antarctica
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The two polar regions are opposites: the Arctic, at the top of the world, is a frozen ocean surrounded by land, while Antarctica, at the bottom, is a continent of land buried under ice and surrounded by ocean.
explain: Polar bears live only in the Arctic and wild penguins only in the southern half of the world, so a cartoon of a polar bear chasing penguins could never happen in nature.
explain: Antarctica is the coldest, windiest, driest and highest continent: its ice is on average about 2 km thick, and if it all melted the seas would rise by about 58 metres. Your freezer at home is about minus 18 degrees; Vostok station once measured about minus 89.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 the far north is mostly blank, low-value land and Antarctica is not even known: it was first sighted in 1820, near the end of the game's period. Yet the Arctic was never empty: Inuit, Sámi, Nenets and other peoples have lived there for thousands of years.
explain: Near the poles the year has strange days: north of the Arctic Circle the summer Sun can shine all night, and in winter it may not rise at all for days.
fact: The Arctic is a frozen ocean surrounded by land, while Antarctica is a continent of land covered by ice and surrounded by ocean.
fact: The ice sheet covering Antarctica is on average about 2 km thick and holds roughly 70 percent of the world's fresh water.
fact: Antarctica is the coldest, windiest, driest and highest continent on average (so little snow or rain falls that it counts as a desert), and the Soviet station Vostok recorded the lowest measured air temperature, about minus 89 degrees Celsius, in 1983.
fact: Under the Antarctic Treaty of 1959, Antarctica is kept for peace and science, with no military bases, and a later agreement bans mining there.
fact: Antarctica has no permanent residents, only scientists and support staff at research stations, from about 1,000 in winter to several thousand in summer.
fact: Antarctica was first sighted in 1820, and Roald Amundsen's Norwegian team reached the South Pole first on 14 December 1911, about a month before Robert Scott's British team, who all died on the way back.
fact: North of the Arctic Circle the Sun stays up for at least one full day in summer and stays below the horizon for at least one full day in winter; at the North Pole itself there are about six months of daylight and six months of dark.
fact: Eight countries have land in the Arctic: Canada, Denmark through Greenland, Finland, Iceland, Norway, Russia, Sweden and the United States.
fact: About four million people live in the Arctic, including Indigenous peoples such as the Inuit in Canada and Greenland, the Sámi in Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia, and the Nenets in Russia.
fact: Since satellite measurements began in 1979, the area of Arctic sea ice at its September low has shrunk by roughly 40 percent, and the Arctic is warming about three to four times faster than the world as a whole: white ice reflects most sunlight back into space, but dark open sea soaks it up, so less ice means more warming.
fact: For centuries Europeans searched for a sea route north of Canada to Asia; Sir John Franklin's expedition of 1845 vanished with all 129 men, and Roald Amundsen first sailed the Northwest Passage between 1903 and 1906.
fact: Polar bears live only in the Arctic and wild penguins only in the southern hemisphere, so they never meet in nature.
exercise: What is the main difference between the Arctic and Antarctica? | The Arctic is a frozen ocean ringed by land; Antarctica is icy land ringed by ocean | The Arctic is land covered by ice; Antarctica is a frozen ocean ringed by land | Both are frozen oceans with no land | Both are hot deserts near the equator
exercise: About how thick is Antarctica's ice on average? | About 2 km | About 2 metres | About 20 km | About 20 cm
exercise: About how much of the world's fresh water is held in Antarctica's ice? | Roughly 70 percent | Roughly 7 percent, less than the Arctic holds | All of it, every drop | Almost none of it
exercise: Where was the lowest air temperature ever measured? | At Vostok station in Antarctica | At the North Pole, in the middle of winter | In Siberia, Russia | On top of Mount Everest
exercise: What does the Antarctic Treaty of 1959 say? | Antarctica is kept for peace and science, with no military bases | Antarctica belongs to Norway, because Norwegians reached the Pole first | Anyone may build an army base there | Antarctica is open for mining by any country that signs it
exercise: Who lives in Antarctica? | Only scientists and support staff at research stations | Penguins and a small Inuit village by the coast, who fish for a living | About four million people in towns | Nobody has ever been there
exercise: Who reached the South Pole first? | Roald Amundsen's Norwegian team, in December 1911 | Robert Scott's British team, in January 1911 | Sir John Franklin, in 1845 | Ernest Shackleton's team, on their ship the Endurance
exercise: When was Antarctica first sighted? | In 1820 | In 1492 | In 1337 | In 1959
exercise: What happens north of the Arctic Circle in midsummer? | The Sun stays up for at least one full day | The Sun never rises at all for a whole month | It is dark for exactly 12 hours | The Sun rises in the west
exercise: Which country has land in the Arctic through Greenland? | Denmark | Germany | Britain | Spain
exercise: How many countries have land in the Arctic? | Eight | Only two | Twenty | None at all
exercise: About how many people live in the Arctic? | About four million | Nobody at all | About four hundred | About four billion
exercise: Which peoples are Indigenous to the Arctic? | The Inuit, the Sámi and the Nenets | The Māori and the Aboriginal Australians | The Aztecs and the Incas | The Zulu and the Maasai
exercise: How much has the Arctic sea ice at its September low shrunk since 1979? | By roughly 40 percent | It has grown by 40 percent | It has not changed at all | It has disappeared completely
exercise: How fast is the Arctic warming compared with the whole world? | About three to four times faster | About half as fast as everywhere else | Exactly the same speed | It is getting colder
exercise: What happened to Sir John Franklin's expedition of 1845? | It vanished with all 129 men | It reached the North Pole and came home | It found a sea route to India | It discovered the coast of Antarctica
exercise: Who first sailed through the Northwest Passage? | Roald Amundsen, between 1903 and 1906 | Sir John Franklin, in 1845, before his ships were lost | Robert Scott, in 1911 | Francis Drake, in 1578
test: A cartoon shows a polar bear hunting penguins. Could this happen in the wild? | No, polar bears live only in the Arctic and wild penguins only in the south | Yes, they meet at the North Pole every winter | Yes, they meet in Antarctica | Yes, but only in summer when the bears swim south across the ocean to find food
test: «antarctica's just a huge iceberg floating on the sea right» What is right? | No, it is a continent of land covered by thick ice | Yes, it is floating ice with nothing under it but water | Yes, like the North Pole | No, it is a ring of islands with no ice
test: «Antarctica's a desert?? it's covered in ice you muppet» Which is the best answer? | It is a desert because very little snow or rain falls there each year | It is not a desert at all | It is a desert because it is hot | It is a desert because there is sand under the ice, just like in the Sahara
test: Why does a white icy surface make the poles even colder? | White ice reflects most sunlight back into space | White ice makes its own cold air | Ice soaks up all the heat of the Sun and stores it deep down | The whiteness blocks the wind
test: What would happen to London if all of Antarctica's ice melted? | It would be flooded, because sea levels would rise by about 58 metres | Nothing, the meltwater would just stay where it is, frozen in the south | London would get drier | The Thames would freeze
test: Why does losing white sea ice make the Arctic warm even faster? | Dark open sea soaks up more sunlight than white ice | Melting ice gives off heat | Polar bears warm the water | The Sun moves a little closer to the Arctic each year
test: In Europa Universalis 5, the far north is shown as nearly empty land. What is wrong with that? | Peoples like the Inuit and Sámi have lived there for thousands of years | Nothing, nobody has ever lived there | Only penguins live there | The north was under the sea then, and the land rose up only a few hundred years ago
test: «scott got to the south pole first innit, the british guy» What is right? | Amundsen's Norwegian team got there first, about a month before Scott | Scott was first by a year | They arrived on the same day and shook hands at the Pole, then went home | Nobody has ever reached the South Pole
test: «how long's the longest day at the north pole then» What is right? | About six months of daylight | Exactly 24 hours | About 12 hours | There is no daylight at all
test: Why do far more people go to Antarctica in summer? | It is light most of the day and a little less cold, so work and travel are possible | The ice melts completely in summer | Summer is the only time it snows | Planes can only fly at night, and summer has the longest and darkest nights
test: Why might countries agree to set aside a whole continent for science? | So that no one fights over it and all can study it in peace | Because nobody wanted any land | Because science is cheaper than mining | Because Antarctica belongs to the United Nations
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Arctic region in contradistinction to the Antarctic? | The Arctic comprises a frozen ocean encircled by land, whereas Antarctica comprises an ice-covered landmass encircled by ocean | The Arctic comprises a continent, whereas Antarctica comprises an ocean | Both regions comprise ice-covered continents | Both regions comprise frozen oceans devoid of land
exam: Identify the principal provision of the Antarctic Treaty concluded in the year 1959. | The continent is reserved for peace and science, and military bases are prohibited thereon | The continent is partitioned among seven claimant states | The continent is designated for commercial mining | The continent is placed under the sovereignty of the Kingdom of Norway
exam: Ascertain which expedition was the first to attain the geographic South Pole. | The Norwegian expedition led by Roald Amundsen, on the fourteenth of December 1911 | The British expedition led by Robert Scott, in the year 1912 | The expedition led by Sir John Franklin, in the year 1845 | No expedition has hitherto attained the South Pole
exam: State the approximate proportion of the world's fresh water retained within the Antarctic ice sheet. | Approximately seventy per cent | Approximately seven per cent | Approximately one hundred per cent | Approximately one per cent
